---
title: File uploads
description: Validate an uploaded file with a File-typed schema field, multipart encoding and Superforms' fail on the server.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

A file input does not expose a text value — it hands the browser a `File` object. Superforms parses that `File` out of the multipart submission, so the schema can validate its presence and size directly.

## Schema

```ts title="src/routes/upload/schema.ts"
import { z } from "zod/v3";

export const uploadSchema = z.object({
	title: z.string().min(1, "Enter a title."),
	attachment: z
		.instanceof(File, { message: "Choose a file." })
		.refine((file) => file.size > 0, "Choose a file.")
		.refine((file) => file.size <= 64_000, "Keep the file at or under 64 kB."),
});

export type UploadData = z.infer<typeof uploadSchema>;
```

## Server

Use `fail` from `sveltekit-superforms` — not SvelteKit's — because SvelteKit's devalue-based serialization cannot encode a `File`. Report the received file with `message` instead of returning it.

```ts title="src/routes/upload/+page.server.ts"
import { fail, message, superValidate } from "sveltekit-superforms";
import { zod } from "sveltekit-superforms/adapters";
import { uploadSchema } from "./schema.js";

export const load = async () => ({
	form: await superValidate(zod(uploadSchema)),
});

export const actions = {
	default: async ({ request }) => {
		const form = await superValidate(request, zod(uploadSchema));

		if (!form.valid) return fail(400, { form });

		// File values never reach the client — Superforms strips them from the returned form,
		// because a file input cannot be repopulated. Report what arrived instead.
		const { name, size } = form.data.attachment;
		return message(form, `Received ${name} (${size} bytes).`);
	},
};
```

## Form

`enctype="multipart/form-data"` makes the file submit without JavaScript; `use:enhance` posts the same `FormData` through fetch, so one form serves both paths. The file input itself stays unbound.

```svelte title="src/routes/upload/+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm, type SuperValidated } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, Field, Label } from "@emmorts/formsnap";
	import type { UploadData } from "./schema.js";

	let { data }: { data: { form: SuperValidated<UploadData> } } = $props();

	const form = superForm(
		untrack(() => data.form),
		{ resetForm: false }
	);
	const { form: formData, enhance, message } = form;
</script>

<form method="POST" enctype="multipart/form-data" use:enhance>
	<Field {form} name="title" fieldErrors>
		<Control>
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label>Title</Label>
				<input {...props} bind:value={$formData.title} />
			{/snippet}
		</Control>
	</Field>

	<Field {form} name="attachment" fieldErrors>
		<Control>
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label>Attachment</Label>
				<input {...props} type="file" accept=".txt,.md" />
			{/snippet}
		</Control>
		{#snippet description()}
			A non-empty file, at most 64 kB. The picker suggests text or Markdown.
		{/snippet}
	</Field>

	<button type="submit">Upload</button>
</form>

{#if $message}
	<output>{$message}</output>
{/if}
```

## How it works

- `z.instanceof(File)` rejects a missing file; the `refine` calls reject empty and oversized ones. All three messages render through the field's `fieldErrors`.
- Superforms strips `File` values from the form it returns to the client, because a file input cannot be repopulated for security reasons. The user's other fields survive a failed submission; the file does not.
- The unbound file input is deliberate: HTML forbids setting a file input's `value`, so there is nothing to `bind:` to.

<Callout type="warning">

Return Superforms' `fail` from `sveltekit-superforms`, not `fail` from `@sveltejs/kit`. The SvelteKit version serializes the action result with devalue, which throws on a `File` — a rejected upload would produce a 500 instead of the validation errors.

</Callout>

<Callout type="note">

`accept=".txt,.md"` is only a file-picker hint. It does not validate anything server-side: the schema above accepts any non-empty `File` up to 64,000 bytes regardless of extension, MIME type or contents. Enforce type rules with your own `refine` if you need them.

</Callout>
